    <p class="prose">
      Two decades stand at opposite poles of the diversity spectrum. The <strong>1940s and 1980s</strong> each produced 12 distinct winning composers &mdash; the joint highest of any decade &mdash; reflecting periods of rapid turnover and stylistic flux. The 1940s saw the studio-system emigres giving way to a new generation, while the 1980s blended holdover legends (Williams, Jarre, Barry) with fresh voices (Vangelis, Moroder, Hancock).
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The <strong>1950s</strong> tell the inverse story: only 7 unique winners across 10 ceremonies, with a repeat-winner rate of 50% &mdash; the highest of any fully elapsed decade. Dimitri Tiomkin alone won three times, and Franz Waxman, Alfred Newman, and Miklos Rozsa all returned to the podium. It was an era of entrenched dominance.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The <strong>2020s</strong>, though only partially complete, are on a striking trajectory: a 71.4% repeat rate driven by Ludwig G&ouml;ransson's three wins in seven ceremonies, alongside repeat appearances by Hans Zimmer, Trent Reznor, and Atticus Ross. Only three first-time winners (Jon Batiste, Volker Bertelmann, Daniel Blumberg) have broken through so far this decade.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The <strong>2010s</strong>, by contrast, were the most open decade in Oscar scoring history: 10 first-time winners in 10 ceremonies, with only Alexandre Desplat's 2017 win for The Shape of Water marking a repeat. No single composer dominated; the decade was a parade of singular achievements.
    </p>
