  <!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS ═══════════════ -->
  <section class="section" id="era-analysis">
    <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Four broad eras, each defined by distinct composer profiles and shifting industry forces</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="eraDistributionChart" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="era-grid">
      <div class="era-card golden">
        <h3>The Golden Age</h3>
        <p class="era-range">1934 &ndash; 1959 &middot; 26 ceremonies</p>
        <p class="era-stat">25 unique composers &middot; 9 repeat wins &middot; 34.6% repeat rate</p>
        <p class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> Max Steiner (3 wins), Dimitri Tiomkin (3), Alfred Newman (2), Franz Waxman (2), Miklos Rozsa (2).<br><br>
          The Golden Age was shaped by European emigre composers who brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. Scores were lush, leitmotif-driven, and inseparable from the studio system that produced them. Steiner, Korngold, and Waxman &mdash; all Vienna-trained &mdash; established the vocabulary of film music itself. The era's repeat rate of 34.6% reflects how a small circle of studio-contracted composers rotated through the category.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card silver">
        <h3>The Silver Age</h3>
        <p class="era-range">1960 &ndash; 1979 &middot; 20 ceremonies</p>
        <p class="era-stat">18 unique composers &middot; 4 repeat wins &middot; 20.0% repeat rate</p>
        <p class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> Maurice Jarre (2), John Barry (2), John Williams (2), Nino Rota (2).<br><br>
          The studio system collapsed, and with it the guarantee of steady composing work. International voices multiplied: Jarre (France), Barry (UK), Rota (Italy), Moroder (Italy), Delerue (France). The scores themselves shifted toward melody as a marketing tool &mdash; Mancini's "Moon River," Jarre's "Lara's Theme." It was the era of the crossover hit, and the repeat rate dropped to 20% as the field broadened.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card modern">
        <h3>The Modern Era</h3>
        <p class="era-range">1980 &ndash; 1999 &middot; 20 ceremonies</p>
        <p class="era-stat">18 unique composers &middot; 7 repeat wins &middot; 35.0% repeat rate</p>
        <p class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> John Barry (2), Alan Menken (3), John Williams (2).<br><br>
          The blockbuster arrived, and with it the return of composer dominance. John Williams' Jaws and Star Wars had already changed the industry; the Modern Era consolidated that shift. Synthesizers entered the conversation (Vangelis, Moroder), jazz crossed over (Hancock), and Disney's animation renaissance powered Alan Menken to three wins in four years. The repeat rate climbed back to 35% as proven names became institutional choices.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="era-card contemporary">
        <h3>The Contemporary Era</h3>
        <p class="era-range">2000 &ndash; 2026 &middot; 27 ceremonies</p>
        <p class="era-stat">22 unique composers &middot; 8 repeat wins &middot; 29.6% repeat rate</p>
        <p class="era-composers">
          <strong>Defining figures:</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson (4), Alexandre Desplat (2), Gustavo Santaolalla (2), Howard Shore (2), Hans Zimmer (2), Trent Reznor (2), Atticus Ross (2).<br><br>
          The most globally diverse era. Winners hail from Sweden (G&ouml;ransson), Argentina (Santaolalla), China (Tan Dun), India (A.R. Rahman), Iceland (Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir), Germany (Bertelmann), and beyond. Genre boundaries dissolved: electronic producers (Reznor/Ross), ambient guitar (Santaolalla), and minimalist percussion (Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir) all won alongside traditional orchestral scores. G&ouml;ransson's four wins &mdash; the fastest accumulation in Oscar history &mdash; signal that the Academy now rewards versatility across genres as much as orchestral craft.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 5: MILESTONES ═══════════════ -->
  <section class="section" id="milestones">
    <h2 class="section-title">Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">Landmark moments, extremes, and anomalies from 93 years of Oscar scoring history</p>

    <ul class="milestone-list">
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1934</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>The first winner.</strong> Louis Silvers wins for <em>One Night of Love</em> at the 6th Academy Awards, establishing the Best Original Score category.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1939</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Largest winning ensemble.</strong> <em>Stagecoach</em> is credited to four composers &mdash; Richard Hageman, W. Franke Harling, John Leipold, and Leo Shuken &mdash; the most in any single win.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1950&ndash;51</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Back-to-back wins.</strong> Franz Waxman wins consecutively for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> &mdash; the first composer to do so in the award's history.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1955</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Longest gap at the time.</strong> Alfred Newman wins for <em>Love Is a Many-Splendored Thing</em>, 11 years after his first win in 1944 &mdash; a record that would stand for decades.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1981</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>The synthesizer arrives.</strong> Vangelis wins for <em>Chariots of Fire</em>, the first primarily electronic score to take the prize &mdash; a watershed for non-orchestral film music.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1986</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Jazz breaks through.</strong> Herbie Hancock wins for <em>Round Midnight</em>, bringing a jazz idiom to a category long dominated by classical and romantic traditions.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1987</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>East meets West.</strong> Ryuichi Sakamoto, David Byrne, and Cong Su share the award for <em>The Last Emperor</em> &mdash; the first win involving an Asian-born composer.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">1989&ndash;92</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Menken's Disney dynasty.</strong> Alan Menken wins three times in four years (The Little Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, Aladdin), the densest win streak by any composer.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2000</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First Asian solo winner.</strong> Tan Dun wins for <em>Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon</em>, the first individual Asian-born composer to take the award.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2008</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Bollywood on the Oscar stage.</strong> A.R. Rahman wins for <em>Slumdog Millionaire</em>, the first Indian composer to receive the award.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2010</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>From rock to Oscar.</strong> Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross win for <em>The Social Network</em>, marking the first time industrial/rock musicians took the prize.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2019</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>First solo female winner.</strong> Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir wins for <em>Joker</em>, the first woman to win Best Original Score as an individual recipient.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2021</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>The 27-year gap.</strong> Hans Zimmer wins for <em>Dune</em>, 27 years after <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) &mdash; the longest gap between wins for any composer in the dataset.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="milestone-year">2026</span>
        <span class="milestone-text"><strong>Four-tie summit.</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson wins for <em>Sinners</em>, joining John Barry and John Williams at four career wins &mdash; and reaching that mark in just eight years (2018&ndash;2026), the fastest ever.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ═══════════════ -->
  <section class="section" id="conclusions">
    <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
    <p class="section-subtitle">What nine decades of data reveal about the trajectory of Oscar-winning film music</p>

    <div class="takeaway-grid">
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-number">1</div>
        <div class="takeaway-text">
          <h4>Composer dominance is cyclical, not linear.</h4>
          <p>The repeat-winner rate swings between extremes: 50% in the 1950s (entrenched dominance), 10% in the 2010s (open field), and an projected 71% in the 2020s (new concentration). There is no steady march toward either monopolization or diversity &mdash; the Oscar landscape oscillates, driven by whether a generational talent is active and whether the Academy's tastes favor continuity or novelty.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-number">2</div>
        <div class="takeaway-text">
          <h4>The podium has globalized, irreversibly.</h4>
          <p>From the 1930s through the 1970s, nearly every winner was either American or a European emigre working within the Hollywood system. Since 2000, winners have come from China, India, Iceland, Argentina, Sweden, Germany, and Japan. The Contemporary Era's 22 unique composers represent the widest geographic spread in Oscar scoring history, and that trend will continue to accelerate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-number">3</div>
        <div class="takeaway-text">
          <h4>The definition of a "score" has expanded beyond recognition.</h4>
          <p>Vangelis' synthesizers (1981), Hancock's jazz (1986), Santaolalla's ambient guitar (2005&ndash;06), Reznor and Ross' industrial textures (2010, 2020), and Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir's cello-driven minimalism (2019) each stretched the boundary of what the Academy recognizes as an "original score." The category now accommodates virtually any sonic palette, provided it serves the film.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-number">4</div>
        <div class="takeaway-text">
          <h4>Speed of accumulation is accelerating.</h4>
          <p>John Barry needed 24 years to reach four wins. John Williams needed 18. Ludwig G&ouml;ransson did it in 8. While the 2023/2024 double entry for Oppenheimer is an anomaly in the data, even excluding it, G&ouml;ransson's pace is unprecedented. In an era where top composers work across franchises, streaming, and film simultaneously, the opportunity to accumulate wins quickly has grown.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-number">5</div>
        <div class="takeaway-text">
          <h4>The 2020s may be shaping a new dynasty.</h4>
          <p>With Ludwig G&ouml;ransson at four wins before the decade ends, Hans Zimmer and Trent Reznor/Atticus Ross each with two, and only three first-time winners so far, the 2020s are on track to rival the 1950s for composer concentration. Whether this tightens further or opens up will depend on the Academy's appetite for rewarding fresh voices versus proven ones &mdash; a tension that has defined the category since 1934.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

